$post_body = <<<'HTML'
<p>The best time to install roller shades in a new home is before move-in day, once drywall and paint are finished but before furniture arrives, since bare windows make measuring and mounting far easier. Late summer and early fall are also a practical window on the calendar, since manufacturing lead times are often shorter than during the spring remodeling rush. The <a href="%BASE%/window-treatments/shades/roller-shades/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">roller shades</a> line at Creative Blinds and Drapes is ordered to the exact window size, so timing the order around the construction schedule matters as much as the season.</p>
%IMAGE1%
<h2 id="what-should-happen-before-the-shades-go-in">What Should Happen Before the Shades Go In?</h2>
<p>A few steps in the right order, each confirmed during a <a href="%BASE%/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Creative Blinds and Drapes</a> measuring visit, prevent delays:</p>
<ul class="bp-list">
  <li>Final paint and trim work should be complete, since drilling into fresh paint can chip the finish.</li>
  <li>Flooring should be in, so installers can set ladders and measure from a finished floor line.</li>
  <li>Windows should be fully cleaned of construction dust before final measurements are taken.</li>
  <li>Any blinds or curtains being removed should come down first so the window opening is clear.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="why-does-the-calendar-season-still-matter">Why Does the Calendar Season Still Matter?</h2>
<p><strong>Beyond the construction timeline, the time of year affects both speed and selection.</strong></p>
<ul class="bp-list">
  <li>Spring and early summer tend to be the busiest season for new-build move-ins, which can stretch installer scheduling.</li>
  <li>Late summer and fall often have shorter order lead times from the manufacturer.</li>
  <li>Installing before the coldest months means roller shades are in place for the first heating season in the home.</li>
  <li>Homeowners moving in winter should order as soon as the home is under contract, since lead times can run several weeks.</li>
</ul>
%IMAGE2%
<h2 id="what-if-the-move-in-date-changes-unexpectedly">What If the Move-In Date Changes Unexpectedly?</h2>
<p>New home closing dates shift often, so it helps to build flexibility into the shade order instead of waiting until the exact date is locked in.</p>
<ul class="bp-list">
  <li>Placing the order once the home passes its final walkthrough avoids most scheduling risk.</li>
  <li>A short delay in closing rarely affects a shade order already in production.</li>
  <li>Asking about expedited production is worthwhile if a move-in date moves up suddenly.</li>
  <li>Leaving a few windows uncovered at first is better than rushing an inaccurate measurement.</li>
  <li>Keeping the measuring appointment on the calendar, even if the move date slips a few days, keeps the project on track.</li>
</ul>
<p>Timing roller shades around the construction schedule instead of a fixed date on the calendar gives the cleanest install and the fewest surprises in a new home. The <a href="%BASE%/guidelines/preparing-your-space/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Preparing Your Space guide</a> walks through how to get each room ready before the measuring appointment, and anyone moving into a new home soon can <a href="%BASE%/contact/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">schedule a free in-home consultation</a> to lock in a measuring date early. For buyers watching the budget, our guide on <a href="%BASE%/blog/what-time-of-year-do-window-blinds-go-on-sale/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">when window blinds typically go on sale</a> covers timing from a pricing angle as well.</p>
HTML;

